            <ArticleTitle>An unexpected but underestimated case of disseminated toxoplasmosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/tid.12818</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Toxoplasma gondii is a ubiquitous intracellular parasite that can cause disseminated infection following reactivation in immunocompromised hosts. We describe a 58-year-old man who died of refractory shock because of disseminated toxoplasmosis. The diagnosis was only made post-mortem on autopsy. We discuss the importance of considering toxoplasmosis on the differential diagnosis in high-risk patients, and review the role of screening and chemoprophylaxis in preventing infection. This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
